    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies neuron-level confidence steering, not superposition, feature overlap, capacity competition, polysemanticity, SAE features, or feature dimensionality. Its own results do not test any listed version.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports sparse role-sensitive neurons and confidence-steering outcomes, recorded below as adjacent findings under none. It does not measure feature geometry or whether features are stored in overlapping directions. Cited results from other studies are excluded. Sparsity and layer sweeps used to select editing settings are not recorded as outcome rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper finds sparse role-sensitive neurons (top 0.5% of mean activation shift) and steers confidence/abstention with them. It does not measure feature overlap, polysemanticity, capacity competition, SAE recovery, or feature dimensionality. It mentions SAEs only in related work, so none of V1–V6 is tested. The record's 'discusses' overstates engagement; 'cites_only' fits better.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that sparse Role-Sensitive Neurons act as a bidirectional gain control for decisiveness/abstention, largely decoupled from factual knowledge. They also conclude that the mechanism is latent from pre-training, with instruction tuning sharpening it. They make no claims about superposition.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper identifies neurons by role-conditioned mean activation shifts and edits a sparse middle-layer vector to steer confidence. This differs from the toy-model superposition method and does not test feature-overlap geometry.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports effects varying with RSN scaling α and edited sparsity; it states that performance peaks around α ≈ 4, low gain is insufficient, and excessive gain introduces noise and over-steering.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Llama3-8B-IT, injecting a sparse mid-layer RSN vector (α=4) cuts Non-Expert abstention from 44.77% to 3.73%. Negative scaling (α=−3) raises it to 65.13%. Zeroing the mid-layer RSNs lowers Non-Expert abstention to 23.25%, while Expert accuracy is unchanged.",
        "proposed_claim": "A sparse set (~0.5%) of role-sensitive mid-layer neurons causally and bidirectionally modulates LLM confidence and abstention.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
          "R2",
          "R3",
